.1619 + 0.9024 + 0.0013 + 3.0697 \\\\\n&\\approx 5.1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test statistic is approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5.1}$.\n\nThere is only one constraint on our data: the total number of observations must equal $N$. Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nν &= number of categories - number of constraints \\\\\n&= 4 - 1 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are\n\n$$\n\\nu = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\ndegrees of freedom.\n\nFrom the given chi-square table, the critical value for $\\nu=3$ at a $5\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\n\\chi^2_{\\textrm{critical}}=7.815\n$$\n\nand the critical region is\n\n$$\nX\\geq 7.815\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fad576b25445e2cadd5862f52f5cd376331349e3ad05e7097be0bfa3fafab8ff.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(5.1)$ does not lie inside the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{insufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161894 1069 "e-9338" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161894 1071 "The salaries of a company's employees range from $\\[math]30\\,000$ to $\\[/math]90\\,000$ dollars. A company director wishes to carry out a hypothesis test with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The salaries are distributed uniformly.\n- $H_1:\\:$ The salaries are not distributed uniformly.\n\nA random sample of employees is taken, and the frequency distribution of their salaries is shown below. Calculate the expected frequency $E_i$ for each salary category.\n\n| Salary (thousands of $\\[math]$) | $30\\leq s_i < 42$ | $42\\leq s_i < 60$ | $60\\leq s_i < 80$ | $80\\leq s_i \\leq 90$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequency $(O_i)$ | $12$ | $10$ | $6$ | $2$ |\n| Expected Frequency $(E_i)$ |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161894 1072 #uu