27\\,$ | $\\,17\\,$ |\n\nConduct a chi-squre goodness -of-fit test at the $5\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The salaries are distributed uniformly.\n- $H_1:\\:$ The salaries are not distributed uniformly.\n\nThe table below gives the values of $w$ that satisfy $P(W\\geq w) = p$, where $W\\sim \\chi^2(\\nu)$.\n\n| $\\nu \\setminus p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $\\,9.210\\,$ | $\\,5.991\\,$ | $\\,4.605\\,$ |\n| $3$ | $\\,11.345\\,$ | $\\,7.815\\,$ | $\\,6.251\\,$ |\n| $4$ | $\\,13.277\\,$ | $\\,9.488\\,$ | $\\,7.779\\,$ |\n| $5$ | $\\,15.086\\,$ | $\\,11.070\\,$ | $\\,9.236\\,$ |" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161895 1072 #uuid "0349d317-e850-55d2-bec8-3b8fadc21180" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161895 1074 "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nLet $Y$ be the salary of a randomly selected employee (in thousands of dollars). Under the null hypothesis, we have that $Y\\sim U[30,90]$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(a\\leq Y\\lt b)= \\dfrac{b-a}{90-30} = \\dfrac{b-a}{60}\n$$\n\nwhere $a \\lt b$ and $a,b\\in [30,90]$.\n\nThe sample size is\n\n$$\nN = 27+49+27+17 = 120\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each class of observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(30 \\le Y < 40) \\\\\n&= 120 \\cdot (\\frac{40 - 30}{60}) \\\\\n&= 20 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(40 \\le Y < 60) \\\\\n&= 120 \\cdot (\\frac{60 - 40}{60}) \\\\\n&= 40 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(60 \\le Y < 80) \\\\\n&= 120 \\cdot (\\frac{80 - 60}{60}) \\\\\n&= 40 \\\\\nE_{4} &= N \\cdot P(80 \\le Y \\le 90) \\\\\n&= 120 \\cdot (\\frac{90 - 80}{60}) \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding expected frequencies is given below:\n\n| Salary (thousands of $\\[math]$) | $30\\leq y_i \\lt 40$ | $40\\leq y_i \\lt 60$ | $60\\leq y_i \\lt 80$ | $80\\leq y_i \\leq 90$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $O_i$ | $\\,27\\,$ | $\\,49\\,$ | $\\,27\\,$ | $\\,17\\,$ |\n| $E_i$ | $\\,20\\,$ | $\\,40\\,$ | $\\,40\\,$ | $\\,20\\,$ |\n| $\\dfrac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i}$ | $\\,2.45\\,$ | $\\,2.025\\,$ | $\\,4.225\\,$ | $\\,0.45\\,$ |\n\nWe compute our test statistic by summing the last row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 2.45 + 2.025 + 4.225 + 0.45 \\\\\n&= 9.15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere is only one constraint on our data: the total number of observations must equal $N$. Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nν &= number of categories - number of constraints \\\\\n&= 4 - 1 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are\n\n$$\n\\nu = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\ndegrees of freedom.\n\nFrom the given chi-square table, the critical value for $\\nu=3$ at a $5\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\n\\chi^2_{\\textrm{critical}}=7.815\n$$\n\nand the critical region is\n\n$$\nX\\geq 7.815\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/62/62ad82a0764f10c5ace9ae36e49c87c10121238f9b94db6062b6b6541352c128.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(9.15)$ lies in the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161896 1069 "e-9341" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161896 1071 "Suppose $Y$ is a random variable with support $S=\\{0,1,2,3, \\ldots\\}$. A random sample is carried out, and the sample data is shown below.\n\n| Observation $(y_i)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\geq 4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequency $(O_i)$ | $30$ | $27$ | $10$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nA chi-square goodness of fit test is conducted at the $10\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\: Y\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$\n- $H_1:\\: Y\\not\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$\n\nUse this data to calculate an estimator for the rate $\\lambda$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161896 1072 #uuid "fd33baa3-d81c-5cc2-8073-6d5255d0da04" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161896 1074 "The number of observations is\n\n$$\nN=30+27+10+3=70\n$$\n\nWe can calculate an estimator for $\\lambda$, which we denote as $\\hat \\lambda$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^(^) &= \\frac{total number of events}{total number of observations} \\\\\n&= (∑(y_{i} \\cdot O_{i}))/(N)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $O_i$ is the observed frequency for the $i$th observation, $N$ is the number of observations. So, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^(^) &= \\frac{0 \\cdot 30 + 1 \\cdot 27 + 2 \\cdot 10 + 3 \\cdot 3}{70} \\\\\n&= 0.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the proposed distribution is $Y\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(0.8)$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161897 1069 "e-9347" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161897 1071 "A publishing house believes the number of misspellings per page of a draft book follows a Poisson distribution $\\mathrm{Po}(2.5)$. They wish to carry out a hypothesis test with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The observed distribution can be modeled as $\\mathrm{Po}(2.5)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ The observed distribution cannot be modeled as $\\mathrm{Po}(2.5)$\n\nThe table below shows the number of misspellings per page and their corresponding frequencies. Calculate the missing expected frequencies. Round your final answers to one decimal place.\n\n| Number of Misspellings $(x_i)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\geq 4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequency $(O_i)$ | $15$ | $18$ | $9$ | $26$ | $12$ |\n| Expected Frequency $(E_i)$ |  | $16.4$ |  |  |  |" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161897 1072 #uuid "f893ad8b-b6ce-5aec-b5f0-f0c19fa6c86e" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161897 1074 "Under the null hypothesis, we have that $X\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(2.5)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = x) &= \\frac{λ^{x}e^{- λ}}{x!} \\\\\n&= ((2.5)^{x}e^{-2.5})/(x!), x = 0, 1, 2, …\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the probability of each missing value of $X{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 0) &= ((2.5)^{0}e^{-2.5})/(0!) \\approx 0.082085 \\\\\nP(X = 1) &= ((2.5)^{1}e^{-2.5})/(1!) \\approx 0.205212 \\\\\nP(X = 2) &= ((2.5)^{2}e^{-2.5})/(2!) \\approx 0.256516 \\\\\nP(X = 3) &= ((2.5)^{3}e^{-2.5})/(3!) \\approx 0.213763 \\\\\nP(X \\ge 4) &= 1 - P(X = 0) - P(X = 1) - P(X = 2) - P(X = 3) = 0.242424\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe number of observations is\n\n$$\nN = 15 + 18 + 9 + 26+ 12 = 80\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{0} &= N \\cdot P(X = 0) = 80 \\cdot 0.082085 \\approx 6.6 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(X = 2) = 80 \\cdot 0.256516 \\approx 20.5 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(X = 3) = 80 \\cdot 0.213763 \\approx 17.1 \\\\\nE_{4} &= N \\cdot P(X \\ge 4) = 80 \\cdot 0.242424 \\approx 19.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding expected frequencies is given below:\n\n| $x_i$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\geq 4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $O_i$ | $15$ | $18$ | $9$ | $26$ | $12$ |\n| $E_i$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6.6}$ | $16.4$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}20.5}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}17.1}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}19.4}$ |" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161898 1069 "e-9351" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161898 1071 "Suppose $Y$ is a continuous random variable with support $S=[0,120]$. A random sample is carried out, and the frequency distribution of the sample data is shown below.\n\n| Class | $0\\leq y_i < 30$ | $30\\leq y_i< 90$ | $90\\leq y_i \\leq 120$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequency $(O_i)$ | $31$ | $42$ | $27$ |\n\nA chi-square goodness of fit test is conducted at the $1\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\: Y\\sim U[0,120]$\n- $H_1:\\:Y\\not\\sim U[0,120]$\n\nCalculate the value of the test statistic for this hypothesis test." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161898 1072 #uuid "2b78e8fc-9d17-5a05-8adb-5e1e8920ddc3" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161898 1074 "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nUnder the null hypothesis, we have that $Y\\sim U[0,120]$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(a\\leq Y\\lt b)= \\dfrac{b-a}{120-0} = \\dfrac{b-a}{120}\n$$\n\nwhere $a \\lt b$ and $a,b\\in [0,120]$.\n\nThe sample size is\n\n$$\nN = 31 + 42 + 27 = 100\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each class of observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(0 \\le Y < 30) \\\\\n&= 100 \\cdot (\\frac{30 - 0}{120}) \\\\\n&= 25 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(30 \\le Y < 90) \\\\\n&= 100 \\cdot (\\frac{90 - 30}{120}) \\\\\n&= 50 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(90 \\le Y < 120) \\\\\n&= 100 \\cdot (\\frac{120 - 90}{120}) \\\\\n&= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding expected frequencies is given below:\n\n| Class | $0\\leq y_i < 30$ | $30\\leq y_i< 90$ | $90\\leq y_i \\leq 120$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $O_i$ | $31$ | $42$ | $27$ |\n| $E_i$ | $25$ | $50$ | $25$ |\n| $\\dfrac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i}$ | $1.44$ | $1.28$ | $0.16$ |\n\nWe compute our test statistic by summing the last row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 1.44 + 1.28 + 0.16 \\\\\n&= 2.88\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the test statistic is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2.88}$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161899 1069 "e-9353" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161899 1071 "A food quality control team tests boxes of packaged salads to check for damage. Each box contains $3$ salad packs. The results of the test are summarized below.\n\n| Number of Damaged Packs in a Box $(x_i)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Frequency $(O_i)$ | $20$ | $30$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n\nA chi-square goodness-of-fit test is conducted at the $1\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The sample data fit a binomial distribution $B(3,p)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ The sample data does not fit a binomial distribution $B(3,p)$\n\nUse this data to calculate an estimate of $p$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161899 1072 #uuid "554461a5-52a5-5c44-b3ce-b1bb42b92cee" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161899 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of damaged packs in a randomly selected box.\n\nFirst, we write down the null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\: X\\sim B(3,p)$\n- $H_1:\\:X\\not\\sim B(3,p)$\n\nThe number of observations is\n\n$$\nN=20+30+8+2 = 60\n$$\n\nWe can calculate an estimate of $p$, which we denote as $\\hat p$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^(^) &= \\frac{total number of successes}{total number of trials} \\\\\n&= (∑(x_{i} \\cdot O_{i}))/(n \\cdot N)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $O_i$ is the observed frequency for the $i$th observation, $N$ is the number of observations, and $n$ is the number of trials in each Binomial sequence. So, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^(^) &= (0 \\cdot 20 + 1 \\cdot 30 + 2 \\cdot 8 + 3 \\cdot 2)/(3 \\cdot (60)) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.2889\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the proposed distribution is $X\\sim B(3,0.2889)$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161900 1069 "e-9470" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161900 1071 "![](images/76/765415456d7f33c3558e08e7c4fc01ef3e80961c1e073a210033d91741d78f60.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s$, where $C$ is the arc of a semicircle of radius $4$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161900 1072 #uuid "55e8e57f-7f9e-529a-aa1b-24e1d86c4133" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161900 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C$, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L\n$$\n\nIn this case, $C$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 4$. Its arc length is given by\n\n$$\nL = πr = 4π\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 4\\pi\n$$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161901 1069 "e-9471" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161901 1071 "![](images/84/84de7754ed9244e3d646e452f6ed6dab349c130d17eae1d4dbc94cb52bd761e1.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s$, where $C$ is the path shown above, traversed once counterclockwise." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161901 1072 #uuid "fdab3229-66ca-59d3-ac20-8a5b3d3506fc" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161901 1074 "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/f4/f4e80a23910612bd55e7578a074f23c76535139889faff479c4dd0521e7496ff.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C = C_1 \\cup C_2 \\cup C_3$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s = \\displaystyle \\int_{C_1}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\displaystyle \\int_{C_2}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\displaystyle \\int_{C_3}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s$.\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C$, i.e.,\n$\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L$.\n\nThe path $C_1$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 3$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1} \\,\\textrm ds = \\dfrac{1}{2}(2 \\pi r) = 3\\pi\n$$\n\nThe paths $C_2$ and $C_3$ are hypotenuses of right triangles whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} \\,\\textrm ds = \\int_{C_3} \\,\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 3\\pi + 5 + 5 = 3\\pi + 10\n$$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161902 1069 "e-9472" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161902 1071 "Consider the curves $C_1$ and $C_2$, defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}C_{1}:r_{1}(t)|= ⟨\\frac{1}{9}t^{3}, t⟩, & 0 \\le t \\le 3 \\\\ C_{2}:r_{2}(u)|= ⟨3u^{3}, 3u⟩, & 0 \\le u \\le 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow suppose $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k$. Which of the following must be true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = 3k$\n2. $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$\n3. $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161902 1072 #uuid "4d5a8f9d-7324-547c-ae5e-b49294debc1b" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161902 1074 "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Notice that $C_1$ and $C_2$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$.\nTo see that $C_1$ and $C_2$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n$x = \\dfrac{1}{9}y^3, \\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 3$.\n- Statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = \\displaystyle \\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161903 1069 "e-9613" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161903 1071 "Find the generating function for the sequence that satisfies the recurrence relation $a_n=3a_{n-1}$ with $a_0=1$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161903 1072 #uuid "afbf5bea-fd6f-5d15-afef-1f0e80a4e31f" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161903 1074 "Let $A$ denote the generating function of our sequence:\n\n$$\nA = 1 + 3x + 9x^2 + 27x^3 + \\cdots\n$$\n\nSince the coefficients of the sequence form a geometric series, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= 1 + 3x + 9x^{2} + 27x^{3} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 + (3x) + (3x)^{2} + (3x)^{3} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= (1)/(1 - (3x)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 - 3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161904 1069 "e-9614" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161904 1071 "Find the generating function for the sequence $3, \\: -1, \\: -6, \\: 2 \\: \\ldots$ that satisfies the recurrence relation $a_n=-2a_{n-2}$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161904 1072 #uuid "f311ec2f-28aa-535b-ac93-4d1e12652d7b" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161904 1074 "For\n\n$$\nn \\geq 2\n$$\n\nour sequence satisfies the relation\n\n$$\na_n +2a_{n-2} = 0.\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nLet $A$ denote the generating function of our sequence:\n\n$$\nA = 3-x-6x^2+2x^3+\\cdots\n$$\n\nThe idea is to manipulate the above expression for $A$ so that we obtain a closed expression instead of an infinite series. We can do this using the recurrence relation $(\\ast)$.\n\nNotice that the coefficient of the term $a_{n-1}$ in $(\\ast)$ is $0$.\nThe coefficient of the term $a_{n-2}$ in $(\\ast)$ is $2$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation for $A$ by $2x^2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2}A &= 6x^{2} - 2x^{3} - 12x^{4} + 4x^{5} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding $A$ and $2x^2A$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= 3 - 0x - 6x^{2} + 2x^{3} + ⋯ \\\\\n2x^{2}A &= 0 - 0x + 6x^{2} - 2x^{3} + ⋯ \\\\\nA + 2x^{2}A &= 3 - 0x + 0x^{2} + 0x^{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the coefficients from the $x^2$ term onward on the right-hand side are constructed from a sum that satisfies the recurrence relation and, therefore, must all be zero. Consequently, we are left with\n\n$$\nA+2x^2A = 3-x\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $A$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA + 2x^{2}A &= 3 - x \\\\\n(1 + 2x^{2})A &= 3 - x \\\\\nA &= \\frac{3 - x}{1 + 2x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161905 1069 "e-9761" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161905 1071 "$(11.1101)_2 =$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161905 1072 #uuid "9ae704de-dce6-5741-9735-da2465085bd4" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161905 1074 "We can write down the binary fraction using a place value chart:\n\n| **Place Values** | $2^1$ | $2^0$ | . | $2^{-1}$ | $2^{-2}$ | $2^{-3}$ | $2^{-4}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Digits** | $1$ | $1$ | . | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, our number in base $10$ can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(10.0101)_{2} &= 1 \\cdot 2^{1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{0}^(⏞)^(whole) + 1 \\cdot 2^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-2} + 0 \\cdot 2^{-3} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-4}^(⏞)^(fraction) \\\\\n&= 2 + 1 + 0.5 + 0.25 + 0 + 0.0625 \\\\\n&= (3.8125)_{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161906 1069 "e-9762" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161906 1071 "$(3.25)_{10} =$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161906 1072 #uuid "a6b151ec-4152-5c6c-84e8-739ae579a306" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161906 1074 "We express the number $(3.25)_{10}$ in base $10$ as follows:\n\n- For the whole number part ($3$), we extract the powers of $2$ from the number, starting from the largest possible one.\n- For the fractional part, we extract the *negative* powers of $2$ from the number, starting from the largest possible one.\n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3.25 &= 3^(⏞)^(whole) + 0.25^(⏞)^(fraction) \\\\\n&= 2^{1} + 2^{0} + 2^{-2} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 2^{1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{0}_(⏟)_(whole) + 0 \\cdot 2^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-2}_(⏟)_(fraction)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding place value chart is as follows:\n\n| **Place Values** | $2^{1}$ | $2^{0}$ | . | $2^{-1}$ | $2^{-2}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Digits** | $1$ | $1$ | . | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(3.25)_{10} = (11.01)_2\n$$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161907 1069 "e-9763" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161907 1071 "Find the first six digits of $(0.9)_{10}$ in base $2$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161907 1072 #uuid "1140d1ae-8754-5021-916d-199e768d8813" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161907 1074 "We extract the negative powers of $2$ from the number, starting from the largest possible one:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.9 &= 0.5_(⏟)_(1/2) + 0.4 \\\\\n&= 0.5_(⏟)_(1/2) + 0.25_(⏟)_(1/4) + 0.15 \\\\\n&= 0.5_(⏟)_(1/2) + 0.25_(⏟)_(1/4) + 0.125_(⏟)_(1/8) + 0.025 \\\\\n&= 0.5_(⏟)_(1/2) + 0.25_(⏟)_(1/4) + 0.125_(⏟)_(1/8) + 0.015625_(⏟)_(1/64) + 0.009375 \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 2^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-2} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-3} + 0 \\cdot 2^{-4} + 0 \\cdot 2^{-5} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-6} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding place value chart is as follows:\n\n| **Place Values** | $2^{-1}$ | $2^{-2}$ | $2^{-3}$ | $2^{-4}$ | $2^{-5}$ | $2^{-6}$ | $\\ldots$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Digits** | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\ldots$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(0.9)_{10} = (0.\\,111\\:001 \\ldots)_2\n$$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161908 1069 "e-9828" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161908 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, find the pseudoinverse $A^+$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161908 1072 #uuid "e0a12130-2908-58fd-a8e0-91083fa30e96" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161908 1074 "If\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V ^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A$, then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV \\, \\Sigma^+ \\, U^T\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Sigma^+$ is the pseudoinverse of $\\Sigma$.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{+} &= VΣ^{+}U^{T} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161909 1069 "e-9829" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161909 1071 "For the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ find the pseudoinverse $A^+$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161909 1072 #uuid "64e7f84f-da34-5ae2-b2d1-72373a1d2888" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161909 1074 "For an \"almost\" zero $m \\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1, \\: \\sigma_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: \\sigma_r\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n \\times m$ matrix with entries\n\n$$\n{\\sigma_1^{-1}}, \\, {\\sigma_2^{-1}}, \\, \\ldots, \\, {\\sigma_r^{-1}}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{+} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}^{+} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6^{-1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4^{-1} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161910 1069 "e-9830" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161910 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r^T$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A$, find the pseudoinverse $A^+$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161910 1072 #uuid "66c34c76-6243-50b8-832f-027abb6ab736" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161910 1074 "If\n\n$$\nA=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r ^T\n$$\n\nis a reduced singular value decomposition of $A$, then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV_r \\, \\Sigma_r^+ \\, U_r^T\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Sigma_r^+$ is the pseudoinverse of $\\Sigma_r$.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{+} &= V_{r}Σ_{r}^{+}U_{r}^{T} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[1] \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot [1] \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 16 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161911 1069 "e-9831" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161911 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W$, and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A \\mathbf{x} = W\\mathbf{b}$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & 18 \\\\ 18 & b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c$?" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161911 1072 #uuid "2acc65a4-62df-5e61-a3c9-cb8a08e1ff8c" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161911 1074 "To get the normal equation corresponding to\n\n$$\nW\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwe pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(WA)^{T} \\cdot WAx &= (WA)^{T} \\cdot Wb \\\\\nA^{T}(W^{T}W)Ax &= A^{T}(W^{T}W)b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 18\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 27\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 40\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}29 \\\\ 54\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+54 = 104\n$$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161912 1069 "e-9832" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161912 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W$, and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ shown above. Find the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b}$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161912 1072 #uuid "22ff2997-c3c3-560e-afdc-52da342dcc10" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161912 1074 "To get the normal equation corresponding to\n\n$$\nW\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwe pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(WA)^{T} \\cdot WAx &= (WA)^{T} \\cdot Wb \\\\\nA^{T}(W^{T}W)Ax &= A^{T}(W^{T}W)b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}21 & -21 \\\\ -21 & 21\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-39 \\\\ 39\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}21 & -21 & -39 \\\\ -21 & 21 & 39\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}21 & -21 & -39 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac{13}{7}+x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{13}{7} + x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161913 1069 "e-9833" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161913 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161913 1072 #uuid "5f949e10-0d73-50c8-9f41-73a0d0f78e29" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161913 1074 "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{x}_i\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_n$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{1}{4}(x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3} + x_{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161914 1069 "e-9834" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161914 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161914 1072 #uuid "bd9fe016-cd5f-5e65-b388-35725ac0fbf4" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161914 1074 "Recall that the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} B B^T\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{1}{3}(x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb_{1} &= x_{1} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nb_{2} &= x_{2} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nb_{3} &= x_{3} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= \\frac{1}{3 - 1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 50\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161915 1069 "e-9835" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161915 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 9 & 4 \\\\ 15 & -7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161915 1072 #uuid "bdef2fdc-0a38-5bfa-9bc6-7ca50e8c10ef" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161915 1074 "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; x_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}x_{2} & ⋯\\end{vmatrix}x_{n}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; b_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}b_{2} & ⋯\\end{vmatrix}b_{n}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}_i = \\mathbf{x}_i - \\mathbf{m}\n$$\n\nfor\n\n$$\ni = 1,2, \\ldots, n\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{m}$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{1}{3}(x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb_{1} &= x_{1} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nb_{2} &= x_{2} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nb_{3} &= x_{3} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161916 1069 "e-9907" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161916 1071 "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Given that the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are $7, 2$, and $0$, find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161916 1072 #uuid "f4a3741e-a87c-5b57-95b3-5ef47f7ad599" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161916 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere\n\n$$\n\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nfrom $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\big\\{A\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖Ax‖^{2} &= (Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\\n&= (x^{T}A^{T})(Ax) \\\\\n&= x^{T}(A^{T}A)x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the matrix $A^T A$.\n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1\n$$\n\nis equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A$.\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ is $7$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left.\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\\\right|^2 \\big\\} = 7 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = \\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{7}$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161917 1069 "e-9908" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161917 1071 "![](images/09/094b6043b9cce7f3ac2a7cc32fa53785d3fbf5b490ce853474f89bb4afa8291a.png)\n\nA $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ are unit eigenvectors of $A^TA$, find an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $36$ of $A^T A$?" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161917 1072 #uuid "06ca6e76-6354-5450-882e-b0c063acd303" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161917 1074 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2\\,\\ldots, \\mathbf v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of $\\mathbb R^n$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A^T A$ with corresponding eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1\\geq \\lambda_2\\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_1$,\n- $\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_2$,\n- $\\cdots$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $\\mid \\mid A\\mathbf v_1 \\mid \\mid = 8 = \\sigma_1$, which means that $\\lambda_1 = 8^2 = 64$, and\n- $\\mid \\mid A\\mathbf v_2 \\mid \\mid = 6 = \\sigma_2$, which means that $\\lambda_2 = 6^2 = 36$.\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = 36\n$$\n\nof $A^T A$ is $\\mathbf{v}_2$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161918 1069 "e-9909" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161918 1071 "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Given that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4$, $\\sigma_2=2$, and $\\sigma_3 = 0$, find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161918 1072 #uuid "23c1671b-da2c-570b-af4f-c6aad258155d" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161918 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere\n\n$$\n\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nfrom $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\big\\{A\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖Ax‖^{2} &= (Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\\n&= (x^{T}A^{T})(Ax) \\\\\n&= x^{T}(A^{T}A)x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix $A^T A$.\n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1\n$$\n\nis equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A$.\n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are called the *singular values* of $A$. Since the largest singular value of $A$ is $\\sigma_1=4$, the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ must be\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=4^2\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\left.\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\\\right|^2 \\big\\} = 4^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sigma_1=4$." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161919 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050960 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162465 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162563 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161919 1039 "Identifying a Multiplication Problem Corresponding to a Model" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186161919 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161722 13194139533366 17592186