39533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160038 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160043 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162476 1069 "q-224404" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162476 1071 "A customer in a casino suspects that a roulette wheel numbered from $1$ to $36$ is biased toward landing on $9$. After alerting the croupier, they inspect the wheel and decide to carry out a hypothesis test.\n\nLet $p$ represent the probability that the roulette lands on $9$. State the null and alternative hypotheses for this test.\n\n$H_{0}$: {{field-1}}\n\n$H_{1}$: {{field-2}}" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162476 1072 #uuid "fbf1a15d-6497-5614-9e62-b9f26f32b732" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160048 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160052 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160057 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162477 1069 "q-224414" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162477 1071 "An email server manager determined that $4%$ of the emails their clients receive are spam. After introducing a new filtering feature, the manager wants to know if the proportion of spam emails her clients receive has decreased. She decides to test the following hypotheses at the $10%$ significance level.\n\n- $H_{0}: p = 0.04$\n- $H_{1}: p < 0.04$\n\nHere, $p$ is the proportion of spam emails.\n\nThe manager samples $50$ client emails and notices that only $1$ is spam.\n\nLet $X$ equal the number of spam emails in a random sample. State the distribution of $X$ under $H_{0}$, the value of $α$, and specify a suitable test statistic for this hypothesis test.\n\nThe distribution of $X$ under the null hypothesis is $X∼$ {{field-1}}.\n\nA suitable test statistic is $P($ {{field-2}} $)$.\n\nThe significance level is $α =$ {{field-3}}" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162477 1072 #uuid "95c080a9-c67f-5a1a-b842-f99fb92a3b0a" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160063 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160066 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160070 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160074 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160080 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160083 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162478 1069 "q-224417" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162478 1071 "An email server manager determined that $4%$ of the emails their clients receive are spam. After introducing a new filtering feature, the manager wants to know if the proportion of spam emails their clients receive has decreased. They decide to conduct a hypothesis test at the $10%$ significance level.\n\nThe manager samples $100$ client emails and notices that only $1$ is spam. The manager compares this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, making use of the following cumulative distribution table for $X∼B(100, 0.04)$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.0169$ | $0.0872$ | $0.2321$ | $0.4295$ | $0.6289$ |\n\nFill in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of spam emails in a random sample of $100$, then a suitable test statistic is\n$X = 1$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nSince $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $10%$, there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.\n\nTherefore, we {{field-6}} that the proportion of spam emails has reduced since the filtering feature was installed." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162478 1072 #uuid "de1db61e-cb2b-52e8-8459-3fa8ff9c2153" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160086 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160089 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160094 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160097 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160100 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160104 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162479 1069 "q-224421" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162479 1071 "A treatment for a particular medical condition has a $0.25$ probability of success. A pharmaceutical company launches a new drug for the condition and claims that the probability of success has improved. A doctor wishes to test the pharmaceutical company's claim, so he conducts a hypothesis test at the $5%$ significance level.\n\nAfter randomly sampling $50$ patients with the condition, the doctor observes that $14$ of them succeed with the new treatment. The doctor compares this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, making use of the following cumulative distribution table for $X∼B(50, 0.25)$.\n\n| $x$ | $12$ | $13$ | $14$ | $15$ | $16$ | $17$ | $18$ | $19$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.5110$ | $0.6370$ | $0.7481$ | $0.8369$ | $0.9017$ | $0.9449$ | $0.9713$ | $0.9861$ |\n\nFill in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of randomly selected patients that are treated successfully, then a suitable test statistic is\n$X = 14$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nSince $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $5%$, there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.\n\nTherefore, we {{field-6}} that the success rate has increased in patients treated with the new drug." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162479 1072 #uuid "5ea1525b-613f-5ae9-b43c-7596747eb567" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160108 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160113 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160116 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160120 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160123 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160126 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162480 1069 "q-224440" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162480 1071 "A light bulb factory's report estimates that $2%$ of the light bulbs produced by its machines are defective. However, a supervisor estimates that this percentage is larger. He conducts a hypothesis test at the $1%$ significance level to test his claim.\n\nAfter randomly sampling $50$ light bulbs, the supervisor notices that $5$ of them are defective. The supervisor compares this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, making use of the following cumulative distribution table for $X∼B(50, 0.02)$.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.7358$ | $0.9216$ | $0.9822$ | $0.9968$ | $0.9995$ | $0.9999$ |\n\nFill in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of randomly selected light bulbs that are defective, then an appropriate test statistic is\n$X = 5$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nSince $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $1%$, there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.\n\nTherefore, we {{field-6}} that the percentage of defective light bulbs is larger than reported." 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162480 1072 #uuid "ce3cab4e-034b-5d03-8e03-2f6e5f825652" 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160130 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160133 13194139533366 17592186045585 true] [17592186162481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160137 13194139533366 175921